window.FIXTURES=window.FIXTURES||{};window.FIXTURES["speech"]={"vid":"hulJBFJUvQM","speaker":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira","role":"Constitutional President","country":"Bolivia","region":"Latin America & Caribbean","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hulJBFJUvQM","date":"20260924","title":"🇧🇴 Bolivia - Constitutional President Addresses United Nations General Debate, 81st Session | #UNGA","seconds":1065,"words":2525,"counts":{"claims":27,"promises":1,"attacks":5},"topics":[{"topic":"Bolivia economy","share":0.2019,"seconds":215},{"topic":"Bolivia democracy","share":0.1046,"seconds":111},{"topic":"Bolivia society","share":0.0845,"seconds":90},{"topic":"Bolivia youth","share":0.0652,"seconds":69},{"topic":"Digitalization","share":0.0647,"seconds":69},{"topic":"Bolivia forests","share":0.0628,"seconds":67},{"topic":"Amazon","share":0.0628,"seconds":67},{"topic":"Bolivia corruption","share":0.0606,"seconds":65},{"topic":"Peace","share":0.0603,"seconds":64},{"topic":"Bolivia narco-terrorism","share":0.0588,"seconds":63},{"topic":"Bolivia mafias","share":0.0585,"seconds":62},{"topic":"Bolivia resources","share":0.0581,"seconds":62},{"topic":"Bolivia lithium","share":0.0572,"seconds":61}],"timeline":[[6,96,"Bolivia society"],[96,173,"Bolivia economy"],[176,245,"Bolivia economy"],[246,315,"Bolivia economy"],[316,379,"Bolivia narco-terrorism"],[380,442,"Bolivia mafias"],[443,505,"Bolivia resources"],[507,572,"Bolivia corruption"],[575,636,"Bolivia lithium"],[637,703,"Bolivia forests"],[704,771,"Amazon"],[774,837,"Bolivia democracy"],[838,902,"Peace"],[903,972,"Bolivia youth"],[974,1042,"Digitalization"],[1043,1092,"Bolivia democracy"]],"stances":{"Bolivia society":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira states that the Bolivian people have endured difficult times, poverty, and crises but maintain unity and a recognition of work.","Bolivia economy":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira attributes Bolivia's current economic crisis to past ideological policies and specific leaders in South America.","Bolivia narco-terrorism":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira asserts that road blockages in Bolivia were financed by narco-terrorism and drug trafficking, resulting in over 20 deaths and a state unable to respond effectively.","Bolivia mafias":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira states that Bolivia is fighting to overcome a crisis caused by criminal organizations that destroy constitutions and democracies, while emphasizing the need for investment and the preservation of the country's people and diversity.","Bolivia resources":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira states that Bolivia possesses strategic natural resources essential for global energy and technological transitions but refuses to export raw materials without adding value domestically.","Bolivia corruption":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira states that the ideology of corruption destroys knowledge and development, and asserts that Bolivia seeks to overthrow this concept while maintaining dignity in international relations.","Bolivia lithium":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira argues that Bolivia seeks partnerships for energy diversification but opposes remaining solely as an exporter of raw materials.","Bolivia forests":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira states that Bolivia has lost forest cover over the last decade and criticizes past leadership for environmental destruction.","Amazon":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira states that Bolivia is implementing laws to protect the Amazon and calls for international agreements with neighboring countries to stop predatory activities.","Bolivia democracy":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira argues that slow multilateral funding procedures disadvantage Bolivia against organized crime and that democracy must be prioritized as a foundation for international relations.","Peace":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira advocates for dialogue and peace techniques over confrontation to protect the poor from the effects of war.","Bolivia youth":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira argues that Bolivia must provide young people with a sense of purpose to drive national transformation and cooperation.","Digitalization":"Rodrigo Paz Pereira states that digitalization is rapidly changing countries and describes his own campaign as a product of this trend, conducted solely through social networks without experts or millions of dollars."},"claims":[{"t":53,"quote":"it is a great honor to be with you for the first time as constitutional president of Bolivia.","topic":"Bolivia society","kind":"fact"},{"t":71,"quote":"a people that has gone through difficult times, a people that knows poverty, but also that knows the meaning of work, that knows division, but also knows how to recognize one another in unity, that has been through economic, political crises, social conflicts, and times of uncertainty","topic":"Bolivia society","kind":"fact"},{"t":163,"quote":"Today, the Bolivian economy uh in 2026 had inflation of more than 20%.","topic":"Bolivia economy","kind":"number"},{"t":222,"quote":"When I took on the challenge of there were $51 million in our reserves because the reserves of the Central Bank had been entirely liquidated.","topic":"Bolivia economy","kind":"fact"},{"t":232,"quote":"Now, 10 months on, we have nearly 1.2 billion we, by the end of the year, are heading towards nearly 4 billion and in the 18 months, nearly 10 billion dollars.","topic":"Bolivia economy","kind":"number"},{"t":248,"quote":"for Bolivia, it is a a record, a milestone, a challenge, and a process of construction to build, strengthen our democracy, and to strengthen our productive model within the country.","topic":"Bolivia economy","kind":"fact"},{"t":274,"quote":"We are ordering our economy. We are putting our house in order. We are facing up to the shortage of fuel. We are rebuilding our We are reforming our institutions.","topic":"Bolivia economy","kind":"fact"},{"t":339,"quote":"53 days. For 53 days, there were blockages of roads in Bolivia, um blocking access to health care and education.","topic":"Bolivia narco-terrorism","kind":"number"},{"t":350,"quote":"More than 20 people died, and this was financed by narco-terrorism and by drug trafficking.","topic":"Bolivia narco-terrorism","kind":"number"},{"t":388,"quote":"those mafias that do not have constitutions, that do not have laws, and do not have to report their resources","topic":"Bolivia mafias","kind":"fact"},{"t":396,"quote":"using organized crime and using resources, be it drug trafficking, smuggling, or gold","topic":"Bolivia mafias","kind":"fact"},{"t":448,"quote":"We make up 60% of the water that the that the Amazon receives comes from One of the greatest cultural wealths of the the continent and the world, and not just from one culture, many cultures within a single nation.","topic":"Bolivia resources","kind":"fact"},{"t":471,"quote":"Each of the cell phones, the mobile phones that you have here in the room 10% of the tin that is in those cell phones comes from Bolivian tin.","topic":"Bolivia resources","kind":"number"},{"t":512,"quote":"In Bolivia, we have discovered and I and we are telling the world, the ideology of corruption is part and parcel of the destruction of knowledge, technology, research, and knowing how to properly make use of capital.","topic":"Bolivia corruption","kind":"fact"},{"t":596,"quote":"The world needs cleaner energy sources, but it also needs minerals, lithium, copper, other critical minerals.","topic":"Bolivia lithium","kind":"fact"},{"t":678,"quote":"In the last 10 years, we've gone from 57% of a territory, which around the world represented forest reserves, to less than 50%","topic":"Bolivia forests","kind":"number"},{"t":701,"quote":"this for the last 20 years they have been destroying it","topic":"Bolivia forests","kind":"fact"},{"t":720,"quote":"Bolivia cannot ask the world to protect our forests if we don't do our fair share as well.","topic":"Amazon","kind":"fact"},{"t":734,"quote":"The protection of the Amazon is a national responsibility, but it is also an international one.","topic":"Amazon","kind":"fact"},{"t":807,"quote":"1 to 3 billion dollars is the value of a democracy in crisis","topic":"Bolivia democracy","kind":"number"},{"t":826,"quote":"we have suffered from the attack of these um narco-terrorists for 53 days again now","topic":"Bolivia democracy","kind":"fact"},{"t":838,"quote":"they wanted confrontation, they wanted blood on the streets. We instead offered dialogue and the technique of peace.","topic":"Peace","kind":"fact"},{"t":852,"quote":"when there is war in the world, those that suffer the most are the poor.","topic":"Peace","kind":"fact"},{"t":908,"quote":"They will be the United Nations Bolivia and countries in the future if we give that meaning to our young people, a meaning and a purpose in their lives, the very reason for being.","topic":"Bolivia youth","kind":"fact"},{"t":917,"quote":"I am and I'm here to be part of building a society.","topic":"Bolivia youth","kind":"self_credit"},{"t":987,"quote":"I am the product of digitalization. I conducted my campaign on social networks alone. I didn't use campaign experts. I didn't use millions of dollars for my campaign.","topic":"Digitalization","kind":"self_credit"},{"t":1062,"quote":"We have agreed to be part of the shield of the Americas, but we have also agreed to be part of the Santiago agreement","topic":"Bolivia democracy","kind":"fact"}],"promises":[{"t":410,"quote":"That is why Bolivia is fighting to overcome this crisis and be able to produce and export","topic":"Bolivia mafias","kind":"promise"}],"attacks":[{"t":132,"quote":"This smells of sulfur.","topic":"Bolivia economy","target":"South American leaders"},{"t":147,"quote":"He turned a traditional leaf into a historic leaf, uh a leaf that is part of our culture. He turned it into a betrayal","topic":"Bolivia economy","target":"Bolivia"},{"t":352,"quote":"this was financed by narco-terrorism and by drug trafficking.","topic":"Bolivia narco-terrorism","target":"Drug traffickers"},{"t":388,"quote":"those mafias that do not have constitutions, that do not have laws, and do not have to report their resources, they are mafias that are using organized crime","topic":"Bolivia mafias","target":"Criminal organizations"},{"t":694,"quote":"In my country, there was a president who talked about the Pachamama, which is God on Earth, and nevertheless, this for the last 20 years they have been destroying it","topic":"Bolivia forests","target":"Bolivia"}],"greece":[],"said":[],"pickup":[],"transcript":[{"t":6,"topic":"Bolivia society","text":"The assembly will hear an address by His Excellency Rodriguez Pas Perera, constitutional president of the Plurinational State of Bolivia. I request protocol to escort His Excellency and invite him to address the &gt;&gt; Señor Presidente &gt;&gt; Mr. President of the Assembly, Mr. Secretary-General, excellencies, ladies and gentlemen, it is a great honor to be with you for the first time as constitutional president of Bolivia. And I would like to begin by talking not about the government, but rather the people, the Bolivian people, the homeland, a people that has gone through difficult times, a people that knows poverty, but also that knows the meaning of work, that knows division, but also knows how to recognize one another in unity, that has been through economic, political crises, social conflicts, and times of uncertainty, but which has never lost sight"},{"t":96,"topic":"Bolivia economy","text":"of something fundamental, and that is the hope of building a better life. I come from a country that today is facing a reality that we cannot hide. But first of all, allow me to say one A part of those 20 years of the reality of a country that has been dragged along is due to the consequences of a time of ideological construction on our In the case of part of uh South America, where one leader came here and said, \"This smells of sulfur.\" It was part of a political and ideological line uh being built upon by another leader who brought a coca leaf here, and he turned a traditional leaf into a historic leaf, uh a leaf that is part of our culture. He turned it into a betrayal, so that construction and development and the vision of a the development of a nation would be Today, the Bolivian economy uh in 2026 had inflation of more than 20%."},{"t":176,"topic":"Bolivia economy","text":"But, it was a country that was an energy power in gas and hydrocarbons and we were reduced to a country that now has to be negotiating with multilateral institutions and with various different nations, and that is not right. They said that they were socialists. carried out a campaign uh 10 months ago, in the 10 months since I've been president, with the concept of of resources, financial capacities, being able to invest and develop. Socialism and ideology does not put food on the table. What does is employment and the development of our economies and our production. When I took on the challenge of there were $51 million in our reserves because the reserves of the Central Bank had been entirely liquidated. Now, 10 months on, we have nearly 1.2 billion we, by the end of the year, are heading towards nearly 4 billion and in the 18 months, nearly 10 billion dollars."},{"t":246,"topic":"Bolivia economy","text":"For many nations, that might not be much, but for Bolivia, it is a a record, a milestone, a challenge, and a process of construction to build, strengthen our democracy, and to strengthen our productive model within the country. I say this clearly, being able to look at the reality in my country, which decided to engage in a change and a transformation and and to not turn back to the past. We are indeed doing this. We are ordering our economy. We are putting our house in order. We are facing up to the shortage of fuel. We are rebuilding our We are reforming our institutions. We are trying to restore internal and external trust in the country. We are doing something that we thought was even more important. We are trying to restore that trust in Bolivians so that we can move forward. Because a nation that does not rebuild its trust cannot move ahead with money alone, with clear rules, with labor, with institutions that work, education, security, and justice."},{"t":316,"topic":"Bolivia narco-terrorism","text":"It's one thing to have a government, which we do have. It's quite another thing to have governability, which we do now have because the uh parliament is drafting and approving laws. And governance is yet another thing. Governance, governability, and governance are not the same things. And in governance, there was a deep-rooted danger that we should denounce in this space of the United Nations. 53 days. For 53 days, there were blockages of roads in Bolivia, um blocking access to health care and education. More than 20 people died, and this was financed by narco-terrorism and by drug trafficking. And a helpless defenseless state that was unable to generate economic and human resources to be able to fight that narco-terrorism, narco-trafficking, which was financing it. Protest and the right to demand a better life is one thing. The right to demand a decent life, better health care, education, and infrastructure."},{"t":380,"topic":"Bolivia mafias","text":"It is quite another thing to want to break the constitutional and democratic framework of our nations. That is why those mafias that do not have constitutions, that do not have laws, and do not have to report their resources, they are mafias that are using organized crime and using resources, be it drug trafficking, smuggling, or gold. It doesn't matter the source of that money. What is important to them is destroying constitutions, destroying democracies, and destroying social stability. That is why Bolivia is fighting to overcome this crisis and be able to produce and export. We want investment, employment, technology. We want resources. We want to integrate into the world. We want to do that while also preserving something that no economic growth can replace, and that is its people, its territory, and its extraordinary biological diversity. Ladies and gentlemen, Bolivia has one specific feature, which is also perhaps an opportunity. We are a country with a small population, but with vast diversity."},{"t":443,"topic":"Bolivia resources","text":"The Andes, the the the Altiplano, the Amazon. We make up 60% of the water that the that the Amazon receives comes from One of the greatest cultural wealths of the the continent and the world, and not just from one culture, many cultures within a single nation. And we have strategic natural resources which could be important for the energy and technological transition for the Each of the cell phones, the mobile phones that you have here in the room 10% of the tin that is in those cell phones comes from Bolivian tin. We have the capacity to provide the world with the resources that it needs to be able to develop a better world. Bolivia does not want to repeat the history of countries that exported their natural resources for decades and remained poor. We do not simply want to extract minerals from the earth so that other countries can add value to them."},{"t":507,"topic":"Bolivia corruption","text":"ideology, and this is very important. I spoke earlier about organized crime and the mafia. In Bolivia, we have discovered and I and we are telling the world, the ideology of corruption is part and parcel of the destruction of knowledge, technology, research, and knowing how to properly make use of capital. The ideology of corruption runs counter to development. It runs counter to our democracies. For that reason, we must overthrow the ideological concept of corruption. We want partners that understand that an investment is not just a transfer of funds, of money. We want a long-term relationship with jobs, training, technology, infrastructure, productivity, a future. That is why Bolivia is open to the world and we want the world to come to Bolivia. But we are open with dignity. Uh not based on demagogical uh speeches based on our past. What Bolivia wants is a firm and strong future respecting our traditions and our original and indigenous peoples, but also giving them opportunities to grow with dignity and to develop our nations."},{"t":575,"topic":"Bolivia lithium","text":"Bolivia wants to uh enter partnerships, to compete, to learn, and it is also open to contributing. This historic moment has seen a discussion that we can no longer postpone. The transition to energy It's not so much a transition in and of itself, but rather diversifying our The world needs cleaner energy sources, but it also needs minerals, lithium, copper, other critical minerals. It needs new supply chains, and it needs to do that in a responsible way, and Bolivia wants to be a part of that conversation. But, we would like to raise a question. Can energy the global energy diversification really be just if the countries that possess strategic resources continue to primarily export them as raw materials? No. That is why we want to enter into partnerships and open ourselves up to the world, but as a society, as partners, and not just as a source of raw materials."},{"t":637,"topic":"Bolivia forests","text":"The energy transition also needs to represent productive diversification. It should create opportunities for producing countries. It should generate local capacities, respect the environment. It should incorporate and include communities, and it should allow our young people to be actors in those new industries. That is why we are proposing a new international relationship around strategic resources. A relationship that is based on investment, knowledge, technology, and added value. The United Nations Well, Bolivia is is a country with one of the greatest forest reserves in the world. In the last 10 years, we've gone from 57% of a territory, which around the world represented forest reserves, to less than 50% of Reforestation and the environment should be a priority for us. In my country, there was a president who talked about the Pachamama, which is God on Earth, and nevertheless, this for the last 20 years they have been destroying it."},{"t":704,"topic":"Amazon","text":"I think the Pachamama has changed its opinion now. It wants to grow and it wants to be taken care of, but it also wants an answer for its original peoples, its indigenous peoples, and the peoples of this region so that they can benefit from this care shown to the Pachamama. Bolivia cannot ask the world to protect our forests if we don't do our fair share as well. That is why we have already implemented various laws and norms which will be part of what Bolivia does among itself and with the world. The protection of the Amazon is a national responsibility, but it is also an international one. We want to conserve the Amazon. And for that reason with neighboring countries that make up that Amazon region, we need to reach agreements. We cannot uh allow continued predating predatory activities on our Amazon, which is the lung of the world. Ladies and gentlemen, there are other challenges which I believe to be inseparable from the future of our nations. The fight against organized crime, as I have already said, organized crime has various different sources of financing."},{"t":774,"topic":"Bolivia democracy","text":"Uh to finance myself, I had to ask multilaterals. I have to ask for resources that come late uh or sometimes never arrive because the slow procedures that those multilateral organizations mean that organized crime has an advantage when it comes to getting from drug trafficking, drug terrorism, or the smuggling of gold to then destabilize our democracies. That is why has to act much more immediately. It cannot be the case that 1 to 3 billion dollars is the value of a democracy in crisis. Democracy is the priority in order to establish relations between our nations. The United Nations is built upon and based upon democracy, based upon respect for laws and and having clear laws, having constitutions. But we have suffered from the attack of these um narco-terrorists for 53 days again now. We have used dialogue and the technique and technology of peace."},{"t":838,"topic":"Peace","text":"They wanted confrontation, they wanted blood on the streets. We instead offered dialogue and the technique of peace. And that same technique of peace which I ask countries at war to use to end confrontations, because when there is war in the world, those that suffer the most are the poor. When there is war in the world, it is the poor that suffer the most with fuel prices, energy, the cost of living. I'm welcome for this opportunity for Bolivia to be with you and to express our concerns. I will conclude talking about Santa Santa Cruz's two departments and Cochabamba as well, which in September, this is the anniversary of those nine departments. But I would also like to express my my fond affection to young Bolivians, young Bolivians just like the the young people in all of the nations of the United Nations needs to find the meaning or and purpose of their lives."},{"t":903,"topic":"Bolivia youth","text":"Our nations need to give our young people that purpose. They will be the United Nations Bolivia and countries in the future if we give that meaning to our young people, a meaning and a purpose in their lives, the very reason for being. I am and I'm here to be part of building a society. That is why in these difficult moments that we are experiencing now, we are working in particular to ensure that young people have a purpose. They want to build the fatherland. They want to build our nation and want to be part of the United Nations as a concept of United Nations so that we can cooperate, work together. And to be firm in that conviction to be able to carry out a form of transformation and bring change to our work as a nation. I believe we need to build a new agenda. An agenda of energy, critical minerals, industrialization, infrastructure, digitalization, water, biodiversity, food security, education, health, gender, science, technology, connectivity, and other things."},{"t":974,"topic":"Digitalization","text":"A new agenda for humanity, and we must debate that and discuss it because changes, digitalization, is changing things very rapidly and changing our ability to understand what is happening in our countries. I am the product of digitalization. I conducted my campaign on social networks alone. I didn't use campaign experts. I didn't use millions of dollars for my campaign. It was only And that is why urgent responses are needed to the challenges facing our nation. Thank you very much for this opportunity given to Bolivia today in this assembly. As I said, not everything has been resolved. We're just getting started. But Bolivia will never be on its knees. It will always stand tall. It will always stand up and be ready to walk forward and move I would like to thank each of the departments in my homeland and tell you that Bolivia is the homeland that never abandons us."},{"t":1043,"topic":"Bolivia democracy","text":"The United Nations should also be the space that never abandons our homelands. This is the space for consolidating our democracy. This is the place where we can consolidate them against organized crime. This is the place where we can strengthen them for development. We have agreed to be part of the shield of the Americas, but we have also agreed to be part of the Santiago agreement to ensure that our democracies have security and certainty and to provide them with progress and with a future. We never give in. Long live the homeland, damn it. Long live Bolivia. Thank you, United Nations. &gt;&gt; On behalf of the assembly, I wish to thank the constitutional president of the Plurinational State of Bolivia."}],"compare":[{"topic":"Digital Governance","share":0.0647,"mean":0.003,"rank":5,"others":7},{"topic":"Latin America","share":0.0628,"mean":0.0044,"rank":8,"others":12},{"topic":"International Law & Order","share":0.0603,"mean":0.0604,"rank":78,"others":102}]};
